Karthik,

Thank you for the note. I have read it twice. I am not able to agree to SCD2
on DIM_CUSTOMER as it stands, and I would rather put the reason in writing now
than have it come out sideways in a design review.

1. The interface for DIM_CUSTOMER runs 4 min 12 sec today. That is the average
of the last thirty runs and it is stable, it does not move much between a
Sunday and a Tuesday.

2. With SCD2 that interface stops being an insert. It has to compare every
incoming row against the current row, close the old row with an end date,
insert the new row and re-point the flag. On an ordinary night the extra time
is nothing worth arguing about. On the nights that matter it is not nothing.
My estimate is 12 to 15 minutes on a month end night. Please note the word
estimate. I have not measured it and I am not pretending I have, it is a
number from experience of this pattern on a table of this shape.

3. Before you tell me that 12 minutes is small, look at the Load Window it is
going into. LP_DAILY_SALES starts at 01:00. Average elapsed is 58 minutes and
on a normal night we are done around 02:05 including the post load checks and
the batch close. P95 is 71 minutes. So on the average night, yes, 12 minutes
is small. The average night is not the one that wakes me up.

4. Month end is the one that wakes me up. On a heavy month end the run has
finished 04:10, 04:12. The availability SLA is 05:30. That is the head room
we are actually working with, and it has been like that for two years, it is
not something the project has caused.

5. We have already breached twice this quarter, 14-Feb and 02-Mar. Both times
I was on a call at 05:00 explaining it and both times the first question was
whether anything new had gone into the load recently. If I put SCD2 in during
April and we breach again in May, the answer to that question is going to be

6. So my question is whether we need SCD2 at all here. Would SCD1 on
DIM_CUSTOMER with a seperate history table not do the same job. Keep the
dimension current and small, write the changed rows out to a history table
with the effective dates sitting on it, and let whoever needs the history
join to that table instead. The nightly cost then sits on a small insert into
a history table and not on a compare and update across the whole dimension.
I am not a modeller and I am willing to be told this is wrong. I would like
to be told why it is wrong, not that it is wrong.

7. I am not saying no. I am saying the window is the window and I am not
putting elapsed time into it on the strength of an estimate, mine or yours.
Measure it on DEV, show me the number and the method, and I will look at it
again the same day. If it is genuinely small I will say so in the review
myself, as per my earlier mail on 13/04 about the scenario naming.

Regards
Farida

